      "math_academy_id": "e-460",
      "problem": "Calculate  $\\displaystyle{\\int 3^{5+4x}\\, \\textrm{d}x }.$",
      "worked_solution": "If we let $u=5+4x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 4\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 4 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {3}^{5+4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {3}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int {3}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{5+4x}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
